\chapter{Numerical Stability Bounds for RSVP Dynamics}

Consider the linearized RSVP equations around a uniform state $\Phi_0, \mathbf{v}_0 = 0$:

\begin{align}
\partial_t \delta\Phi &amp;= D_\Phi \nabla^2 \delta\Phi - \beta \delta\Phi \\
\partial_t \delta\mathbf{v} &amp;= D_v \nabla^2 \delta\mathbf{v} - \beta \delta\mathbf{v} + \nabla \delta\Phi
\end{align}

Fourier analysis yields the dispersion relation:

\[
\lambda(\mathbf{k}) = -D_\Phi k^2 - \beta + \frac{|\mathbf{k}|^2}{D_v k^2 + \beta}
\]

The system is stable if $\Re[\lambda(\mathbf{k})] &lt; 0$ for all $\mathbf{k}$. The critical instability occurs when:

\[
\max_k \left( -D_\Phi k^2 - \beta + \frac{k^2}{D_v k^2 + \beta} \right) = 0
\]

Solving yields the numerical stability bound:

\[
\boxed{\beta &lt; \beta_{\text{num}} = \frac{D_\Phi D_v \pi^4}{L^4 + D_v \pi^2 L^2}}
\]

This provides a concrete computational threshold: for given grid resolution $L$ and diffusion coefficients, optimization pressure above $\beta_{\text{num}}$ will generate spurious instabilities in discrete simulations.

