\section{Historical Arc of Behavioral Capture}

The trajectory from pre-digital persuasion to algorithmic capture spans over a century. Edward Bernays, applying Freudian psychology to mass manipulation in the 1920s, formalized propaganda as the "engineering of consent" \cite{bernays1928propaganda}. The Cambridge Analytica scandal of 2018 revealed how microtargeted political advertising weaponized behavioral data at scale. TikTokâ€™s algorithm, by 2023, demonstrated real-time preference shaping through continuous engagement loops.

We model the acceleration of capture intensity as:
\begin{equation}
\beta(t) = \beta_0 \exp(\alpha t^2)
\end{equation}
where $\beta_0$ is baseline susceptibility and $\alpha &gt; 0$ captures the quadratic acceleration due to data compounding and algorithmic refinement. Empirical proxies include:
- Average daily screen time (proxy for $L$, cultural diameter)
- Click-through rate variance (proxy for $\nabla \Phi$)
- Kullbackâ€“Leibler divergence between user behavior and platform prediction

