\begin{abstract}
Across journalism, public discourse, and critical technology studies, Jacob Wardâ€™s warning that AI and behavioral science will conspire to automate choice has emerged as one of the most incisive diagnoses of emergent algorithmic power. However, Wardâ€™s framework remains sociological in ontology and local in mechanism: it names the dangers but cannot formalize them as dynamical collapse modes, nor distinguish centralized authoritarian capture from multi-polar entropic fragmentation.

This document reconstructs Wardâ€™s thesis through the Relativistic Scalar--Vector Plenum (RSVP), a field-theoretic model of collective cognition in which semantic possibility evolves as a scalar entropy field $\Phi(x,t)$ and agency propagates as a vector field $\mathbf{v}(x,t)$. We demonstrate that ``a world without choices'' corresponds to degenerate field topology: $\nabla\Phi\to 0$ (semantic gradient collapse) and monopolar vector divergence $\nabla\cdot\mathbf{v} \gg 0$ (agency centralization), a state we define as \textit{entropic authoritarianism}.

The manuscript contributes:
(1) a full formalization of behavioral capture as field collapse with Eckhaus stability analysis,
(2) deep integration with Friston's Free Energy Principle, Maturana/Varela autopoiesis, and hydrodynamic nonreciprocity,
(3) rigorous numerical stability bounds and active matter cognition analogs,
(4) new design invariants for entropy-positive AI, and
(5) a reconstruction of the intellectual lineage leading to Wardâ€™s claims, extended into a unifying thermodynamic ontology of governance, cognition, and power.
\end{abstract}

